          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"flaubert-madame-bovary-fad5eca3365af663\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":1,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":1,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.9,\"analysis\":\"Emma’s “Je t’aime!” is principally a direct avowal of her love for Rodolphe and also explains her impulsive visit. Its enactment of commitment is weak: the affair already includes vows, but this sentence does not itself clearly undertake a new obligation. The unexpected substitution of a love declaration for a practical answer gives slight support to affective overflow, though the utterance remains adequately understood mainly as an emotionally intense avowal.\",\"ambiguity\":\"The exclamation and embrace do not independently establish E. Slight E support comes instead from the declaration’s affect-driven, pragmatically indirect response to Rodolphe’s questions; it could still be interpreted entirely as an emphatic T avowal.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"direct\",\"description\":\"Emma directly says “Je t’aime!” to Rodolphe upon arriving unexpectedly at his room and puts her arms around his neck.\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"Emma is exhilarated by the newly consummated affair and models her experience partly on the adulterous heroines of her reading. Her declaration appears sincere as an avowal of her present infatuation, while also operating as an indirect explanation of why longing drove her to make the risky dawn visit. The surrounding vows and established lover relationship provide limited commitment-related coloring, but the sentence itself does not clearly renew or formulate an undertaking. Narrative irony about Emma’s romantic scripts and Rodolphe’s later annoyance complicates the relationship without changing the utterance’s dominant T force.\",\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“— Je t’aime! répondit-elle en lui passant les bras autour du cou.”\",\"supports\":\"Direct affirmative avowal strongly supports T; the embrace and exclamation establish emotional intensity but only weakly bear on E.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Rodolphe asks how she managed to come and remarks that her dress is wet; Emma answers with “Je t’aime!”\",\"supports\":\"The pragmatically indirect answer presents love as the motive for her visit and gives slight evidence that affect is producing or displacing an ordinary practical response.\",\"confidence\":0.9},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Immediately before the visit, Emma is “prise par la fantaisie de voir Rodolphe à l’instant,” and the idea makes her pant with longing.\",\"supports\":\"Supports the sincerity and affective urgency surrounding the avowal, while not by itself establishing expressive/reflexive core force.\",\"confidence\":0.97},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The lovers had already made vows, and Emma had asked Rodolphe to repeat that he loved her.\",\"supports\":\"Provides weak relational and commitment-related context for P, but also indicates that love declarations function within their affair as avowals and reassurance rather than necessarily as new undertakings.\",\"confidence\":0.94},{\"evidence_id\":\"e5\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The narration describes Emma’s long-contained love as bursting forth joyfully and links her self-conception to literary heroines.\",\"supports\":\"Explains the declaration’s intense sincerity and romantic self-dramatization without creating an aspect of core force outside T/P/E.\",\"confidence\":0.95}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T/P/E naturally represents the utterance: it is dominantly an avowal, with weak commitment coloring and slight possible expressive overflow. Emma’s literary self-fashioning and the novel’s irony belong to contextual interpretation rather than requiring O.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":null}"
        }
